{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>You imported one function. The bundle grew by 80KB anyway</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      <code>import { debounce } from 'utils-lib'</code>. One function. But the built bundle jumped by 80KB, roughly the size of the entire library. The bundler didn't remove the 40 other functions you never touched.
    </p>
    <p>
      This isn't a bundler bug. The bundler is being cautious on purpose, because it genuinely can't prove those other 40 functions are safe to delete.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Without an explicit signal, a bundler has to assume any module-level code in a file might have a side effect, something that runs and matters even if nothing imports the value it produces, like registering a global, patching a prototype, or running analytics on import. Deleting "unused" code that might do that would be an unsafe, incorrect optimization.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Tree shaking is fundamentally a proof problem: the bundler must prove an export is unreachable and side-effect-free before removing it, and JavaScript's dynamic nature makes that proof genuinely hard to construct automatically for most real code.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Tell the bundler which files are actually safe to prune</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      <code>"sideEffects": false</code> in a package's <code>package.json</code> is a promise from the library author: none of these files do anything meaningful just by being imported. With that promise, the bundler can safely drop any export nothing actually uses.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      If a package genuinely does have a few side-effectful files (a CSS import, a polyfill that patches a global), list them explicitly instead of marking the whole package: <code>"sideEffects": ["./src/polyfill.js", "*.css"]</code>. Everything not listed is fair game for removal.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This only helps if the library is also authored as real ES modules (not transpiled to CommonJS as the primary entry point), static <code>import</code>/<code>export</code> syntax is what lets the bundler statically analyze reachability at all, a CommonJS <code>require()</code> graph is opaque to this analysis.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The utility library that quietly doubled a bundle</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team's marketing site imported a single date-formatting helper from a general-purpose utility package. Nobody thought to check the bundle report, until a routine Lighthouse audit flagged a huge JS payload on a page that visibly only formatted one date string.
    </p>
    <p>
      The utility package had no <code>sideEffects</code> field at all. By default, that means "assume side effects everywhere," the safest possible assumption for the bundler, and the worst possible one for bundle size.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The team couldn't change the library's own package.json (it was a third-party dependency), so they added a per-import override in their bundler config declaring that specific package side-effect-free, verified by actually reading through its source for anything that ran on import, and found nothing that did.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      They also added a bundle-analyzer step to CI afterward specifically to catch this class of regression automatically, since the failure mode is invisible in code review, a reasonable-looking one-line import that happens to drag in an entire library looks identical to a genuinely small import until someone measures the actual output.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

